  "questionLabels": [],
  "question": {
    "text": "The total number of molecular orbitals formed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> atomic orbitals of a diatomic molecule",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "08",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>2s and 2p atomic orbitals combine, forming σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals. σ orbitals result from head-on overlap, while π orbitals arise from sideways overlap. This molecular orbital theory explains the bonding in molecules</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn></mn></math><mo> </mo><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> orbital will form, two molecular orbitals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>.<br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> orbital will form six molecular orbitals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">σ</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></msub></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mo>*</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub></math></p><p>Hence the total number of molecular orbitals formed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> atomic orbitals of a diatomic molecule formed is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
